fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1008", description: some(value: "FOUND WHILE DRIVING WI-978 (see docs/proposals/059-secondary-entries.md R2). An\noperation declared in a SECONDARY ENTRY backs a spec claim at LOAD TIME but is\nUNREACHABLE by the requires-dictionary DISPATCH route at run time: the program\nloads clean and dies. That is the WI-818 failure mode, in the placement 059 R2\nmakes the whole point of the mechanism.\n\nMEASURED, three fixtures differing only in where the backing op sits. Spec:\n`sort Show { sort T = ?; operation show(x: T) -> Int64 }`. Caller:\n`sort Caller { sort T = ?; requires Show[T = T]; operation useIt(x: T) -> Int64 = show(x) }`.\n\n  (1) op in the SORT BODY, provision in the sort body\n      => loads clean, 1 provision, `Rec.show` = 111, `Caller.useIt` = 111\n      <- CONTROL, works\n\n  (2) op in a SECONDARY ENTRY (`namespace Rec { operation show(x: Rec) = 111 }`),\n      provision ONE LEVEL OUT\n      => loads clean, 1 provision, `Rec.show` = 111,\n         `Caller.useIt` = EvalError::OperationBodyMissing { name: \"…Show.show\" }\n\n  (3) op AND provision both in the secondary entry\n      => identical to (2)\n\nSo the PROVISION placement is not the discriminator -- (2) and (3) fail alike\nwhile (1) passes. The OP placement is. `op_backed` (typing.rs,\n`check_provider_operations`) finds the secondary entry's `show` and accepts the\nclaim; the runtime dictionary route does not, and reports the SPEC op as missing\na body.\n\nNOT ESTABLISHED, and the decision this ticket has to make: which side is wrong.\n\n  (a) WIDEN dispatch -- the requires-dictionary route should see a secondary\n      entry's members, since 059 R2 says what a secondary entry declares IS a\n      member of the sort and may back its claims. Note the DIRECT call already\n      dispatches (`Rec.show` = 111 in (2)), so membership is partly wired\n      already; find which reader the dictionary route consults instead.\n\n  (b) NARROW `op_backed` -- refuse a secondary entry's op as backing, so the\n      LOAD is refused rather than the run. This CONTRADICTS 059 R2 and would\n      make the mechanism unable to supply members for a foreign carrier, which\n      is most of why one writes a secondary entry. Named for completeness, not\n      recommended.\n\nWI-978 fixed the neighbouring EMITTER defect (a claim in a secondary entry to a\nfree-standing `entity` recorded no provision at all, so no reader saw it) and\ndeliberately did not reach this: different subsystem, and (a)/(b) is a design\ncall. WI-978's own test file states the split at its head.\n\nACCEPTANCE: a test DRIVES the generic caller through the requires path with the\nbacking op declared in a secondary entry and asserts the VALUE -- not that it\nloads. Fixture (1) is included unchanged as the both-sides control, since it\npasses either way and is what attributes a failure to the placement rather than\nto the fixture. State at the test site which of the three fixtures fails when\nthe fix is backed out. cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-06T07:12:02Z"))
